---
layout: layouts/base.njk
pagination:
  data: activeLangs
  size: 1
  alias: lang
pageSlug: "events"
pageTitle: events
---
{# NOTE: This page is live at /en/events/ and /hr/events/ but hidden from
   the nav until site.json -> "showEvents" is set to true. When real events
   exist, replace the empty state below with event cards + registration. #}
{% set t = i18n[lang] %}
<header class="page"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="cue foam rise">{{ t.events.cue }}</div>
  <h1 class="page-h1 rise-2">{{ t.events.h2_thin }} <b>{{ t.events.h2_bold }}</b></h1>
</div></header>

{#- BONES, NOT THE DESIGN. The spec keeps the visual pass for later — the
   invitation anatomy, the one interactive moment, the alternating record. This
   renders the collection honestly in the site's existing classes so the data
   can be seen and edited long before any of that is decided. Replacing this
   markup later changes nothing behind it. -#}
{#- THE INVITATION, THE RECORD, AND THE HONEST WAIT.

   Three permanent parts, and the page keeps this shape whether it holds one
   gathering or twenty. It never graduates into a calendar or a list, because
   a calendar is what you look at when you already intend to go and this page
   exists to make somebody want to.

   ANATOMY BORROWED FROM A WEDDING INVITATION, deliberately: an announcement
   line, then who and what stated plainly, then the date and place set apart
   typographically but QUIET, then the action small and bounded at the end. An
   invitation does not shout its RSVP button; it tells you something is
   happening and trusts you.

   THE DATE IS THE OBJECT. An invitation prints the date large, because the
   date is the thing somebody has to decide about — everything else is detail
   they read once they are already interested. It used to sit in a WHEN/WHERE
   facts row at 16px, smaller than the summary above it, which is how a
   spreadsheet states a date rather than how an invitation offers one.

   ONE INTERACTIVE MOMENT, not scattered decoration. A light draws along the
   card's top edge as it arrives and the date rolls in beneath it, per
   character, using the same cascade the section cues and the page wheel use —
   the site's own motion vocabulary rather than a new trick for one page. It
   runs once, and nothing at all happens under prefers-reduced-motion. -#}
<section><div class="wrap">
  {%- set upcoming = [] %}{% set past = [] %}
  {%- for g in gatherings %}
    {%- if g.status == "upcoming" %}{% set _ = upcoming.push(g) %}
    {%- elif g.status != "canceled" %}{% set _ = past.push(g) %}{% endif %}
  {%- endfor %}

  {#- ONE INVITATION, NOT ONE PER GATHERING. The spec features THE next one,
     and the difference matters: three cards each announcing "You are invited"
     is a list wearing an invitation's clothes, and the announcement stops
     meaning anything by the third time somebody reads it. Anything else
     already scheduled is named quietly underneath, which is what an invitation
     to one evening does when the host has a second date in mind. -#}
  {#- `slice` in Nunjucks CHUNKS a list into N lists rather than taking the
     first n of it, which quietly rendered every gathering as the featured one.
     Naming the item is clearer than any filter would have been. -#}
  {% if upcoming.length %}
    {% set g = upcoming[0] %}
    {%- set title = g.title[lang] or g.title.en or g.title[g.langs[0]] %}
    {%- set summary = g.summary[lang] or g.summary.en %}
    <article class="invite is-next">
      {% if g.photo %}<div class="invite-photo"><img src="{{ g.photo }}" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div>{% endif %}

      <div class="invite-card">
        <p class="invite-cue">{{ t.events.invited }}</p>

        {#- THE DATE AND THE NAME, side by side on a wide card, stacked on a
           narrow one. Two columns because the card is as wide as the page and
           a single column left half of it empty whenever the gathering had no
           photograph to fill the other side. -#}
        <div class="invite-head">
          {% if g.date %}
          <p class="invite-when">
            {#- The label is for a screen reader only. A date set this large,
               directly under "you are invited", does not need the word WHEN
               in front of it — and printing one is the interface explaining
               itself instead of working. -#}
            <span class="vh">{{ t.events.when_label }}</span>
            {#- ONE STRING, NOT day / month-year split. Croatian, Serbian and
               Slovenian put the month in the genitive when a day precedes it
               ("14.-15. ozujka"), so splitting the parts to stack them would
               print correct English and broken Croatian. The filter already
               knows each locale's whole form; this keeps it. -#}
            <span class="invite-date">{{ g.date | when(g.end_date, lang) }}</span>
            {% if g.time %}<span class="invite-time">{{ g.time }}</span>{% endif %}
          </p>
          {% endif %}

          <div class="invite-lede">
            <h3 class="invite-title">{{ title }}</h3>

            {#- Said where somebody is about to read words that are not theirs,
               rather than hiding the gathering from them. A Croatian-only entry
               is proof of real work, not a gap to apologize for. -#}
            {% if not g.title[lang] %}<p class="invite-lang">{{ t.events.other_lang }}</p>{% endif %}

            {% if summary %}<div class="invite-blurb">{{ summary | md | safe }}</div>{% endif %}
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="invite-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

        {#- WHERE, unlabeled for the same reason the date is: a place name
           under a date reads as the place. The cohort facts keep their labels,
           because "every week" and "12" do not say what they are on their
           own. -#}
        {% if g.location %}
        <p class="invite-where">
          <span class="vh">{{ t.events.where_label }}</span>
          <a href="{{ g.map_url }}" data-map="{{ g.location }}"
             target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ g.location }}</a>
        </p>
        {% endif %}

        {% if g.type == "cohort" and (g.cadence or g.capacity) %}
        <dl class="invite-facts">
          {% if g.cadence %}
          <div><dt>{{ t.events.often_label }}</dt>
            <dd>{{ t.events["cadence_" + g.cadence] or g.cadence }}</dd></div>
          {% endif %}
          {% if g.capacity %}
          <div><dt>{{ t.events.places_label }}</dt><dd>{{ g.capacity }}</dd></div>
          {% endif %}
        </dl>
        {% endif %}

        {% if g.sessions.length %}
        <ol class="invite-sessions">
          {% for s in g.sessions %}
          <li><span class="invite-session-date">{{ s.date | when(null, lang) }}</span>{{ s.topic }}</li>
          {% endfor %}
        </ol>
        {% endif %}

        {#- SMALL AND BOUNDED. Never the visual star of the page: the point of
           an invitation is the thing being offered, not the button. -#}
        {% if g.registration %}
        <p class="invite-act">
          <a href="{{ g.registration }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ t.events.register }}</a>
          {% if g.application_required %}<span class="invite-note">{{ t.events.application }}</span>{% endif %}
        </p>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
    </article>

    {#- ALSO AHEAD. Same card, no announcement line, no moment — quieter on
       purpose, so the featured one keeps being the featured one. -#}
    {% if upcoming.length > 1 %}
    <div class="also">
      <p class="cue bare also-cue">{{ t.events.also_cue }}</p>
      {% for g in upcoming %}{% if not loop.first %}
      {%- set title = g.title[lang] or g.title.en or g.title[g.langs[0]] %}
      <article class="also-item">
        <p class="also-when">{{ g.date | when(g.end_date, lang) }}{% if g.location %} · {{ g.location }}{% endif %}</p>
        <h3>{{ title }}</h3>
        {% if g.summary[lang] or g.summary.en %}<p>{{ (g.summary[lang] or g.summary.en) | md | safe }}</p>{% endif %}
        {% if g.registration %}
        <p class="invite-act"><a href="{{ g.registration }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ t.events.register }}</a></p>
        {% endif %}
      </article>
      {% endif %}{% endfor %}
    </div>
    {% endif %}
  {% else %}
  {#- THE EXPECTED LONG-TERM CONDITION, not a degraded fallback. This page may
     hold nothing for years, and saying "check back later" would be both untrue
     and a little embarrassed about it. It states the vision and names what has
     to mature first, in the same register the Mission page uses. -#}
  <div class="empty invite-waiting">
    <h3>{{ t.events.empty_title }}</h3>
    <p>{{ t.events.empty_text }}</p>
    <div class="workline"><i></i></div>
    <a class="btn ghost" href="/{{ lang }}/contact/">{{ t.events.empty_cta }}</a>
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  {#- THE RECORD. Alternating sides down the page so it reads as a story being
     told rather than a spreadsheet with pictures — and so that the page has
     something to show a supporter long before it has anything to invite
     anyone to. -#}
  {% if past.length %}
  <div class="record">
    <p class="cue bare record-cue">{{ t.events.past_cue }}</p>
    {% for g in past %}
    {%- set title = g.title[lang] or g.title.en or g.title[g.langs[0]] %}
    {%- set summary = g.summary[lang] or g.summary.en %}
    <article class="record-band{% if loop.index0 % 2 %} is-flipped{% endif %}">
      <div class="record-photo">
        {% if g.photo %}<img src="{{ g.photo }}" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
        {% else %}<span class="record-noshot" aria-hidden="true"></span>{% endif %}
      </div>
      <div class="record-body">
        <p class="record-when">{{ g.date | when(g.end_date, lang) }}{% if g.location %} · {{ g.location }}{% endif %}</p>
        <h3>{{ title }}</h3>
        {% if summary %}<div>{{ summary | md | safe }}</div>{% endif %}
      </div>
    </article>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>
  {% endif %}
</div></section>